# BrainBox — environment
#
# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored; this file is not, so never put
# a real value in here.
#
# Two of these groups you set yourself. The database group Vercel writes for
# you once the Neon integration is attached — see "On Vercel" at the bottom.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- database ---
# Neon Postgres. Two URLs, because they are not interchangeable:
#
#   DATABASE_URL          pooled (-pooler in the host). Every request the app
#                         serves goes through this. Serverless opens a lot of
#                         short connections, and the pooler is what stops Neon
#                         running out of them.
#   DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED direct. Migrations only. Schema changes need a real
#                         session, and the pooler cannot give one.
#
# Get both from the Neon dashboard: Connection Details, then toggle "Pooled
# connection". Keep ?sslmode=require on both.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://user:password@ep-example-123456-pooler.eu-central-1.aws.neon.tech/brainbox?sslmode=require"
DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED="postgresql://user:password@ep-example-123456.eu-central-1.aws.neon.tech/brainbox?sslmode=require"


# -------------------------------------------------------------------- auth ---
# Auth.js (NextAuth v5).
#
# AUTH_SECRET signs the session cookie. Generate one per environment — local
# and production must NOT share it:
#
#   npx auth secret          (or: openssl rand -base64 32)
AUTH_SECRET="generate-me-do-not-copy-this-value"

# Where the app answers. On Vercel this is detected from the deployment, so it
# is only needed locally. Set it if you ever see a callback redirect to the
# wrong host.
AUTH_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# At least one sign-in provider. Auth.js v5 picks these up by name — the
# AUTH_<PROVIDER>_ID / _SECRET convention means no wiring in code.
#
# GitHub: Settings > Developer settings > OAuth Apps > New OAuth App.
#   Homepage       http://localhost:3000
#   Callback URL   http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/github
# Add a second OAuth app for production with the deployed URL — one app cannot
# hold two callback URLs.
AUTH_GITHUB_ID=""
AUTH_GITHUB_SECRET=""

# Optional second provider. Google Cloud Console > Credentials > OAuth client.
#   Callback URL   http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/google
AUTH_GOOGLE_ID=""
AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET=""


# ------------------------------------------------------------------- rooms ---
# Upstash Redis, holding online room state.
#
# Rooms currently live in a globalThis Map, which works under one node process
# and cannot work on Vercel: a player joining may land on an instance that has
# never seen the room. Redis is the shared place every instance reads.
#
# The REST pair, not the redis:// URL — the REST client works from serverless
# without holding a socket open.
# Upstash console > your database > REST API.
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL="https://example-12345.upstash.io"
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN="paste-the-token-from-the-upstash-rest-api-tab"


# --------------------------------------------------------------------- app ---
# Absolute URL of this deployment. Used to build shareable room links, which
# have to be absolute to be worth sharing.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL="http://localhost:3000"


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# On Vercel
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Add these by hand, to Production / Preview / Development:
#
#   AUTH_SECRET                 a DIFFERENT value from your local one
#   AUTH_GITHUB_ID              from the production OAuth app
#   AUTH_GITHUB_SECRET          from the production OAuth app
#   UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL
#   UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL         https://<your-project>.vercel.app
#
# Do NOT add by hand:
#
#   DATABASE_URL, DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED
#     Vercel writes these itself once you attach the Neon integration
#     (Project > Storage > Create Database > Neon). Adding them manually as
#     well gives you two sources of truth, and a stale one the day you branch
#     the database.
#
#   AUTH_URL
#     Detected from the deployment. Setting it pins every preview deployment to
#     one host and breaks OAuth on the others.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ is a publishing keyword: anything with that prefix is compiled
# into the browser bundle and is readable by anyone. Only NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL
# belongs there. If a key is ever given that prefix, treat that key as leaked.
